@node 1 Overview
@chapter 1. Overview
@anchor{#1-overview}
imud is a general-purpose IMU daemon --- @emph{gpsd for IMUs}. It owns
the inertial sensor and does the real-time work once: it reads a
gyroscope/accelerometer (IMU) and a magnetometer over I²C or SPI, fuses
them with a Multiplicative Extended Kalman Filter (MEKF) into a
real-time attitude estimate (quaternion, Euler angles, magnetic and true
heading, rate of turn, heave), and publishes the result on several
standard interfaces that any number of programs can consume at once.
Consumers get a fused, timestamped estimate rather than raw samples to
process themselves.

The same daemon serves marine navigation (NMEA 0183, true heading,
heave), robotics (binary attitude), machine vision and camera
stabilization (high-rate quaternion with hardware timestamps), and
gimbal/pointing systems. Output is use-agnostic; a few fusion options
have marine defaults (noted where they apply).

@strong{Threads.} A reader thread drains the IMU FIFO on a GPIO
watermark interrupt; a second reads the magnetometer; a fusion thread
runs the MEKF at the full IMU rate; a health thread serves
@code{imud-status} and pets the systemd watchdog; optional output
threads emit NMEA, binary, and a local subscription stream; an optional
position thread pulls GPS fixes from gpsd or SignalK to keep magnetic
declination current. See
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/spec.md,spec.md
§3} for the full architecture.

@strong{Dependencies.} C11, POSIX, @code{libgpiod} (1.x or 2.x,
auto-detected), and the C standard library. Nothing else.

