@node Prerequisites
@section Prerequisites
@anchor{#prerequisites}
@itemize
@item
A Linux system with an I²C @strong{or} SPI bus and a GPIO character
device. Debian bookworm and trixie are the packaged targets (arm64 and
armhf); Raspberry Pi OS is the most exercised host, not a requirement.
@item
The bus you intend to use, enabled:
@itemize
@item
@strong{I²C} --- on Raspberry Pi OS @code{sudo raspi-config} → Interface
Options → I²C. Elsewhere, load @code{i2c-dev} and confirm a
@code{/dev/i2c-*} node appears.
@item
@strong{SPI} --- @code{sudo raspi-config} → Interface Options → SPI, or
add @code{dtparam=spi=on} to @code{/boot/firmware/config.txt} and
reboot. Confirm @code{/dev/spidev0.0} and @code{/dev/spidev0.1} appear.
Not every driver supports SPI; see
@ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}.
@end itemize

@item
@code{libgpiod-dev}. Bookworm ships 1.6.x and trixie ships 2.x; both are
supported and the Makefile auto-detects the installed version via
@code{pkg-config}.
@end itemize

@verbatim
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y build-essential libgpiod-dev
@end verbatim

@node Build
@section Build
@anchor{#build}

@verbatim
make            # builds imud, imud-cal, imud-imutest, imud-status, imud-mon
make test       # builds and runs the host-side unit tests (no hardware needed)
@end verbatim

@code{make test} must be run from the repository root --- one test loads
@code{data/WMM.COF} by relative path.

@node Install
@section Install
@anchor{#install}

@verbatim
sudo make install
@end verbatim

This installs:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.22 0.78 
@headitem 
Item
 @tab Destination
@item 
Binaries
 @tab @code{/usr/local/bin/}
@item 
Reference config
 @tab @code{/etc/imud/imud.conf} (skipped if it already exists)
@item 
Calibration file
 @tab @code{/etc/imud/cal.json} (if @code{config/cal.json} is present)
@item 
systemd unit
 @tab @code{/etc/systemd/system/imud.service}
@item 
Client libraries
 @tab @code{libimud.so} + @code{/usr/local/include/imud.h} (see
@code{man 3 libimud}), @code{/usr/local/share/imud/imud_client.py}
@item 
Man pages
 @tab @code{imud.8}, @code{imud-cal.8}, @code{imud.conf.5},
@code{imud-status.1}, @code{libimud.3}
@end multitable

@code{imud-mon} and @code{imud-imutest} share their own install target.
They are operator tools rather than part of the running system:
@code{imud-mon} reads the UDP streams and can live on any machine on the
network, while @code{imud-imutest} talks to the sensor directly --- over
whichever bus is configured --- and must run on the daemon's own box,
with the daemon stopped.

@verbatim
sudo make install-utils
@end verbatim

WMM coefficient data is installed separately --- it is versioned by
model epoch (WMM2025), not by imud release, so it ships as its own
package (@code{imud-wmm-data}) that can be updated independently:

@verbatim
sudo make install-wmm-data
@end verbatim

This installs @code{WMM.COF} → @code{/usr/share/imud/WMM.COF}. To use a
newer model before the package updates, drop it at
@code{/etc/imud/WMM.COF} --- imud prefers that path when it exists.

@code{make install} also creates the dedicated system user @code{imud}
that the service runs as. Its @strong{primary group is @code{imud}},
which owns @code{/run/imud/} and both AF_UNIX sockets; hardware access
comes from the supplementary groups @code{gpio} (for
@code{/dev/gpiochip*}) and @code{i2c} (for @code{/dev/i2c-*}). Those two
groups are @strong{created} if missing --- Raspberry Pi OS ships them,
stock Debian does not, and @code{imud.service} will not start without
them. A udev rule (@code{/etc/udev/rules.d/60-imud.rules}, or
@code{/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/} from the @code{.deb}) gives those groups
access to the device nodes, which is what Raspberry Pi OS provides in
its own @code{99-com.rules}.

To read the stream socket or run @code{imud-status} as an ordinary user,
join the @code{imud} group --- not @code{gpio}:

@verbatim
sudo adduser "$USER" imud     # log out and back in for it to take effect
@end verbatim

@code{sudo make uninstall} reverses the install but preserves
@code{/etc/imud/} (your config and calibration).

Override install locations with the usual variables, e.g.
@code{sudo make install PREFIX=/opt/imud ETCDIR=/opt/imud/etc}.
Packagers also override @code{UDEVDIR} (default
@code{/etc/udev/rules.d}) --- udev reads rules only from
@code{/etc/udev/rules.d}, @code{/run/udev/rules.d} and
@code{/usr/lib/udev/rules.d}, never from @code{PREFIX}.

