@node In the foreground testing
@section In the foreground (testing)
@anchor{#in-the-foreground-testing}

@verbatim
imud --config config/imud.conf
@end verbatim

To try the full pipeline with no hardware, use the bundled simulation
config, which selects the @code{sim} driver for both sensors:

@verbatim
imud --config config/sim.conf
@end verbatim

@node As a systemd service
@section As a systemd service
@anchor{#as-a-systemd-service}

@verbatim
sudo systemctl enable --now imud     # start now and on boot
sudo systemctl status imud
journalctl -u imud -f                # follow the log
@end verbatim

The unit is @code{Type=notify} and hardened
(@code{ProtectSystem=strict}, @code{NoNewPrivileges},
@code{DevicePolicy=closed} with explicit @code{DeviceAllow} lines,
@code{MemoryMax=32M}, a 10 s systemd watchdog). The shipped unit allows
every supported node --- @code{/dev/i2c-1}, @code{/dev/i2c-3},
@code{/dev/spidev0.0}, @code{/dev/spidev0.1}, @code{/dev/gpiochip0} and
@code{/dev/gpiochip4} --- so it needs no edit on either a Pi 4 or a Pi
5, on either transport; an allow for a node the board does not have is
inert. Only the config needs to match your board
(@code{device.gpio_chip}, @code{device.i2c_bus}, and @code{spi_dev} if
you use SPI).

The daemon runs as @code{imud} with
@code{SupplementaryGroups=gpio i2c spi}. All three groups are created by
the package's maintainer scripts (or @code{make install}), and
@code{60-imud.rules} gives them the device nodes --- Raspberry Pi OS
already does this in its own @code{99-com.rules}.

If you point @code{i2c_bus}, @code{spi_dev} or @code{gpio_chip} at a
node outside that list --- SPI1..6, or a second SPI bus --- add a
matching @code{DeviceAllow=} line to the unit. Under
@code{DevicePolicy=closed} the open is refused otherwise, and the
failure looks like a permissions problem rather than a policy one.
@code{make check-devices} compares the shipped config against the
shipped unit and fails the build if they disagree, which is what stops
the two drifting apart.

All six units --- the daemon and the five bridges --- additionally carry
an empty @code{CapabilityBoundingSet=},
@code{SystemCallArchitectures=native}, a
@code{SystemCallFilter=@@system-service} allow-list minus
@code{@@privileged} and @code{@@resources}, @code{RestrictNamespaces},
@code{LockPersonality}, @code{RestrictSUIDSGID}, @code{ProtectHostname},
and @code{ProtectProc=invisible} with @code{ProcSubset=pid}. Together
those put every unit at OK under @code{systemd-analyze security}.

One deliberate exception: @strong{@code{imud.service} does not set
@code{ProtectClock=}}, and re-allows @code{adjtimex} after its
@code{~@@privileged} line. The daemon calls @code{adjtimex} read-only at
startup to report the CLOCK_TAI offset; @code{ProtectClock=} blocks that
call outright, with no way to allow it back, which would turn the check
into a false "chrony has not set tai_offset" warning on every boot.
Setting the clock is already impossible without CAP_SYS_TIME, which the
empty @code{CapabilityBoundingSet=} removes. The five bridges never call
@code{adjtimex} and do set @code{ProtectClock=true}.

@code{imud.service} runs at @code{UMask=0027} and the bridges at
@code{0077}; the daemon tightens an inherited umask but never loosens
it. Both AF_UNIX sockets (@code{/run/imud/imud.sock} and the stream
socket) are created at mode 0660 rather than chmod'd down after
@code{bind()}, so they are never momentarily wider --- however
@code{imud} was launched, systemd or not.

@node Command-line options
@section Command-line options
@anchor{#command-line-options}

@verbatim
imud [OPTIONS]
  --config PATH      Config file (default: /etc/imud/imud.conf)
  --replay FILE      Replay an .imucap capture through the full pipeline
                     (forces both sensors onto the sim driver, plays the file
                     once and exits; docs/capture.md)
  --skip-bias-cal    Skip the startup gyro-bias estimation window
  --no-nmea          Disable the NMEA output stream
  --no-highrate      Disable the high-rate binary stream
  --foreground       Accepted and ignored (imud always runs in the foreground)
  --version          Print the version and exit
  -h, --help         Print this help and exit
@end verbatim

If @code{--config} is omitted, imud tries @code{/etc/imud/imud.conf} and
then @code{~/.config/imud/imud.conf}. A config file that exists but
contains a bad value is fatal --- imud reports every error and refuses
to start rather than run on a half-applied config. A missing file is not
an error (built-in defaults are used).

@node Signals
@section Signals
@anchor{#signals}
@multitable @columnfractions 0.26 0.74 
@headitem 
Signal
 @tab Effect
@item 
@code{SIGHUP}
 @tab Reload the config. @code{[hot]} fields take effect immediately;
@code{[restart]} fields are ignored. The log file, if configured, is
reopened (for logrotate). If the file fails to parse, the running config
is kept.
@item 
@code{SIGTERM}, @code{SIGINT}
 @tab Clean shutdown: emit a final binary packet with
@code{FLAG_SHUTDOWN}, stop and join all threads, remove the PID file and
sockets.
@end multitable

Reload with @code{sudo systemctl reload imud} or
@code{kill -HUP $(pidof imud)}.

