@node 4 Configuration reference
@chapter 4. Configuration reference
@anchor{#4-configuration-reference}
imud is configured with a single TOML-like text file.

@strong{Default locations.} @code{--config /path/to/imud.conf} names the
file outright; without it the daemon searches, first match wins:

@enumerate 
@item
@code{/etc/imud/imud.conf} (installed by @code{make install})
@item
@code{~/.config/imud/imud.conf}
@end enumerate

@code{--config} @emph{replaces} the search rather than heading it. A
path named there that does not exist means built-in defaults and a
warning --- not a quiet fall back to @code{~/.config/imud/imud.conf},
which would let a typo start the daemon on a different configuration.
Without @code{--config}, neither file existing is fine.

@strong{Reload behaviour:}

@itemize
@item
Keys marked @strong{[restart]} take effect only after restarting the
daemon (@code{systemctl restart imud}).
@item
Keys marked @strong{[hot]} can be reloaded at runtime by sending
@code{SIGHUP}:

@verbatim
sudo systemctl reload imud   # or: kill -HUP $(pidof imud)
@end verbatim

Unknown keys and bad values in a SIGHUP reload are logged as warnings;
the previously loaded values are kept.
@item
A reload re-reads @strong{the file startup actually loaded}, including
when that was the @code{~/.config/imud/imud.conf} fallback, and it
starts from the built-in defaults before applying that file --- exactly
as startup does. So @strong{deleting a [hot] key from the file restores
its default} on the next reload, instead of leaving the running value in
place until a restart; reload and restart agree.
@item
If the config file has since been deleted, the running configuration is
kept. A deleted config does not revert a running daemon to defaults.
@end itemize

@strong{Syntax:}

@itemize
@item
Comments start with @code{#} and may appear inline.
@item
Strings must be double-quoted: @code{driver = "ism330dhcx"}.
@item
Integers may be decimal (@code{833}) or hex (@code{0x6B}). An integer
too large for the platform's @code{int} is a fatal error, not silently
wrapped --- @code{dest_port = 4294977414} is refused rather than
becoming port 10118. Keys with a meaningful range are checked against
it: ports @code{1}--@code{65535}, @code{int_gpio} @code{0}--@code{255}
(@code{0} means the sensor has no interrupt line), @code{i2c_addr}
@code{0x00}--@code{0x7F}.
@item
Booleans: @code{true} / @code{false}.
@item
Arrays: @code{[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]} (used by @code{rotation_euler_deg} and
@code{rotation_matrix}). The element count is checked exactly.
@item
@code{~/} paths are expanded to @code{$HOME/}.
@item
A string value longer than the field it sets is a fatal error, and so is
one that fits only until @code{~/} expands. imud never shortens a value
and continues: a truncated socket or file path is a perfectly valid path
to the wrong place, and every client would then connect to the path you
actually wrote and find nothing there.
@end itemize

The annotated reference file at @code{config/imud.conf} documents every
key inline and is the fastest way to see a working configuration.

