@node [device]
@section [device]
@anchor{#device}
Hardware bus and GPIO controller. @strong{[restart]}

@multitable @columnfractions 0.15 0.09 0.19 0.56 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{i2c_bus}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"/dev/i2c-1"}
 @tab I²C bus device node. Use @code{/dev/i2c-1} on Pi 4;
@code{/dev/i2c-1} or @code{/dev/i2c-3} on Pi 5 depending on which header
pins are used.
@item 
@code{gpio_chip}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"gpiochip0"}
 @tab gpiochip device name. @strong{Run @code{gpiodetect} and use the
chip it lists for the header pins} --- the number comes from probe order
and is not stable across kernels. @code{"gpiochip0"} is right on Pi 4,
and on Pi 5 with kernels from mid-2024 onward (which renumber the RP1
controller to 0 like every other model). Earlier Pi 5 kernels exposed it
as @code{"gpiochip4"}, and Pi OS keeps a @code{/dev/gpiochip4} symlink
for compatibility --- a symlink, not a second controller.
@item 
@code{sim_file}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{""}
 @tab An @code{.imucap} capture for the sim driver to replay
(@code{driver = "sim"} in both @code{[imu]} and @code{[mag]}); empty
selects the built-in synthetic scenario. @code{imud --replay FILE} is
the shortcut. See
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/docs/capture.md,capture
& replay}.
@item 
@code{sim_loop}
 @tab bool
 @tab @code{false}
 @tab Repeat the capture forever; timestamps and sequence numbers are
rebased to stay monotonic. Ignored under @code{--replay}, which plays a
file once and exits.
@item 
@code{sim_speed}
 @tab float
 @tab @code{1.0}
 @tab Playback pacing: @code{2.0} = double speed, @code{0} = as fast as
the pipeline accepts.
@end multitable

@node [imu]
@section [imu]
@anchor{#imu}
IMU (gyroscope + accelerometer) driver settings. @strong{[restart]}

@multitable @columnfractions 0.19 0.09 0.19 0.53 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{driver}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"ism330dhcx"}
 @tab Driver to load. See @ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}.
@item 
@code{bus}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"i2c"}
 @tab Transport: @code{"i2c"} or @code{"spi"}. SPI is faster per
transfer, which is what unlocks the high sample rates and shortens the
FIFO drain; not every driver has it, and the daemon refuses to start on
one that does not (see @ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}).
The IMU and the magnetometer choose independently.
@item 
@code{spi_dev}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{""}
 @tab spidev node, e.g. @code{"/dev/spidev0.0"} (CE0). @strong{Required}
when @code{bus = "spi"}; ignored otherwise. The chip select in the node
name does the addressing, so @code{i2c_addr} is unused.
@item 
@code{spi_speed_hz}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0}
 @tab SPI clock in Hz. @code{0} means the driver's datasheet maximum,
which is the useful default. A request above that maximum is clamped
rather than refused, and the daemon logs what it really programmed.
@strong{Do not set this below 2.5 MHz when a magnetometer shares the
same SPI controller.} Measured on an ism330dhcx + mmc5983ma pair:
driving the IMU below about 2.2 MHz stops the magnetometer completing
measurements entirely, and re-running @code{init()} cannot hold it. The
default is unaffected, and the daemon warns at startup when it sees a
slow clock alongside a shared-bus magnetometer.
@item 
@code{i2c_addr}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0x6B}
 @tab I²C address; used only when @code{bus = "i2c"}. @code{0x6B} (SA0
high) or @code{0x6A} (SA0 low via jumper).
@item 
@code{int_gpio}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{17}
 @tab BCM GPIO number for the FIFO watermark interrupt (board pin 11).
Set @code{0} to poll instead of using a hardware interrupt. The polling
cadence is then the same @code{fifo_wm + int_grace} sample periods the
interrupt path waits for, unless @code{poll_ms} overrides it: a flat
cadence under-polls at high ODR, where the FIFO overflows and the
effective rate drops below the configured one, and burns reads at low
ODR.
@item 
@code{odr_hz}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{833}
 @tab Output data rate in Hz; must be greater than zero. A rate the chip
cannot produce is rounded @strong{up} to the next one it can, and the
filter is tuned for that actual rate --- the daemon logs
@code{requested, N Hz actual} at startup when the two differ. ISM330DHCX
supports: @code{13.016}, @code{26.031}, @code{52.063}, @code{104.125},
@code{208.25}, @code{416.5}, @code{833}, @code{1666}, @code{3332},
@code{6664}. Other drivers differ --- see the driver table in
@ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}, and note that the top
rates of some parts are beyond what a Raspberry Pi can sustain.
@item 
@code{accel_g}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{8}
 @tab Accelerometer full-scale range in g. ISM330DHCX: @code{2},
@code{4}, @code{8}, @code{16}.
@item 
@code{gyro_dps}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{2000}
 @tab Gyroscope full-scale range in degrees/second. ISM330DHCX:
@code{125}, @code{250}, @code{500}, @code{1000}, @code{2000},
@code{4000}.
@item 
@code{fifo_wm}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{64}
 @tab FIFO watermark in sample-sets: how deep the chip buffers before
raising its interrupt. This sets @strong{both buffer depth and sample
latency} --- with @code{int_grace}, the reader waits
@code{fifo_wm + int_grace} sample periods, so 64 at 833 Hz batches about
77 ms. On the ST parts a watermark of 8 or more also batches the chip
timestamp into the FIFO; at 32 and above that costs 1.6% of the word
budget, so the same watermark holds about 63 sample-sets rather than 64.
@item 
@code{int_grace}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{2}
 @tab How late the interrupt may be, in @strong{samples}, before the
reader gives up and reads anyway. The wait is @code{fifo_wm + int_grace}
sample periods, so the fallback fires only when the line is genuinely
@emph{late} --- never merely because the batch is not ready yet. Counted
in samples rather than milliseconds because a fixed time means a
different thing at every rate: 2 ms is thirteen samples at 6664 Hz and
three hundredths of one at 13 Hz. Ignored when @code{int_gpio = 0},
where there is no expected arrival to be late against.
@item 
@code{poll_ms}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0}
 @tab Polling cadence in milliseconds for an install with @strong{no}
interrupt line. @code{0} derives it from the rate and watermark, which
is normally what you want: a fixed interval under-polls at high ODR ---
the FIFO overflows and the delivered rate falls below the configured one
--- and burns reads at low ODR. Set a non-zero value only to force a
specific cadence. @strong{Ignored entirely when @code{int_gpio} is
non-zero.}
@end multitable

@node [mag]
@section [mag]
@anchor{#mag}
Magnetometer driver settings. @strong{[restart]}

@multitable @columnfractions 0.19 0.09 0.18 0.54 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{driver}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"mmc5983ma"}
 @tab Driver to load. See @ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}.
@item 
@code{bus}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"i2c"}
 @tab Transport: @code{"i2c"} or @code{"spi"}, as for @code{[imu]}
above. The AKM compasses (@code{ak09916}, @code{ak8963}) are I²C-only
--- they have no SPI port and are reached through the host IMU's bypass.
@item 
@code{spi_dev}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{""}
 @tab spidev node, e.g. @code{"/dev/spidev0.1"} (CE1 --- the IMU usually
takes CE0). @strong{Required} when @code{bus = "spi"}.
@item 
@code{spi_speed_hz}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0}
 @tab SPI clock in Hz; @code{0} means the driver's datasheet maximum. As
for @code{[imu]}. Note that a magnetometer's @emph{own} clock is not
what starves it on a shared controller --- a slow
@code{[imu] spi_speed_hz} is. This key can be lowered safely.
@item 
@code{i2c_addr}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0x30}
 @tab I²C address; used only when @code{bus = "i2c"}. MMC5983MA has a
fixed address. The AKM compasses inside a 9-axis IMU --- AK09916 in the
ICM-20948, AK8963 in the MPU-9250/9255 --- sit behind the host chip's
I²C @strong{bypass}, not its I²C master, and answer on the host bus at
their own address: set @code{0x0C} for both.
@item 
@code{int_gpio}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{27}
 @tab BCM GPIO number for the measurement-done interrupt (board pin 13).
Set @code{0} to poll on a timer.
@item 
@code{odr_hz}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{100}
 @tab Output data rate in Hz; must be greater than zero. Rounded
@strong{up} to a supported rate as for @code{[imu] odr_hz}, and the mag
noise variance is sized for that actual rate. MMC5983MA supports:
@code{1}, @code{10}, @code{20}, @code{50}, @code{100}, @code{200},
@code{1000}.
@item 
@code{set_period_s}
 @tab float
 @tab @code{5.0}
 @tab Interval in seconds between SET/RESET degauss pulses. Prevents
gradual magnetisation of the sensor. Set @code{0} to disable.
@item 
@code{int_grace}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{2}
 @tab How late the interrupt may be, in @strong{samples}, before the
reader gives up and reads anyway. These magnetometers have no FIFO, so
the line signals one finished conversion and the wait is
@code{1 + int_grace} sample periods, so the fallback fires only when the
line is genuinely @emph{late} --- never merely because the batch is not
ready yet. Counted in samples rather than milliseconds because a fixed
time means a different thing at every rate: 2 ms is thirteen samples at
6664 Hz and three hundredths of one at 13 Hz. Ignored when
@code{int_gpio = 0}, where there is no expected arrival to be late
against.
@item 
@code{poll_ms}
 @tab int
 @tab @code{0}
 @tab Polling cadence in milliseconds for an install with @strong{no}
interrupt line. @code{0} derives it from the rate, which is normally
what you want: a fixed interval under-polls at high ODR --- the FIFO
overflows and the delivered rate falls below the configured one --- and
burns reads at low ODR. Set a non-zero value only to force a specific
cadence. @strong{Ignored entirely when @code{int_gpio} is non-zero.}
@end multitable

@node [fusion]
@section [fusion]
@anchor{#fusion}
MEKF noise parameters and tuning knobs. @strong{[hot]}

Applied live on SIGHUP. The @code{mekf_*} noise densities are
@strong{tuned constants}, not raw sensor readouts:
@code{mekf_gyro_noise} and @code{mekf_gyro_bias} build the filter's
process noise Q, held deliberately above the measured sensor floor so
the filter stays responsive and the gyro bias observable. The per-unit
noise that @code{imud-cal characterize} measures (cal.json @code{noise}
section, see
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/docs/capture.md,capture.md})
is informational only and never supersedes them --- driving Q from the
raw floor makes the filter too stiff. The knobs most worth touching for
a noisy install are @code{mag_reject_gauss}, @code{accel_skip_thresh},
@code{mag_yaw_only}, @code{heave_tau_s}, and @code{wave_tau_s}.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.34 0.09 0.13 0.44 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{mekf_gyro_noise}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.007}
 @tab Gyro noise density in rad/s/√Hz. ISM330DHCX datasheet: 7 mdps/√Hz
≈ 0.000122 rad/s/√Hz.
@item 
@code{mekf_gyro_bias}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.00015}
 @tab In-run gyro bias instability in rad/s. Sets the bias random-walk
process noise.
@item 
@code{mekf_accel_noise}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.0022}
 @tab Accelerometer @strong{sensor} noise density in m/s²/√Hz.
ISM330DHCX: ~186 µg/√Hz × 9.81. Not the knob for rough weather --- see
@code{mekf_wave_accel}.
@item 
@code{mekf_mag_noise}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.0004}
 @tab Magnetometer noise density in Gauss/√Hz. MMC5983MA: 0.4 mGauss
RMS.
@item 
@code{mekf_wave_accel}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.8}
 @tab Steady-state σ, in m/s², of the wave-orbital acceleration
contaminating the gravity measurement. @strong{This is the knob for "the
sea is rough."} The disturbance is correlated over roughly a second
rather than white, so it is carried as a first-order Gauss--Markov
state; without it the filter treats 833 strongly correlated samples per
second as 833 independent measurements of gravity and diverges. @code{0}
(or @code{mekf_wave_accel_tau_s = 0}) disables the state and restores
the pre-1.7 6-state filter exactly. Too small is the failure mode that
hurts, so round up; @code{imud-cal fit-ra} measures it from a capture.
@item 
@code{mekf_wave_accel_tau_s}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.5}
 @tab Correlation time in seconds of the Gauss--Markov wave-acceleration
state. @code{0} disables. @strong{Unrelated to @code{wave_tau_s}}, which
is the sea-state reporting window. Attitude error and wave acceleration
are told apart only by their dynamics, so too long a value lets the
state absorb genuine tilt: benchmark yaw-only attitude RMS degrades 2.3°
at 0.5 s → 7.1° at 2 s. Short is the safe side.
@item 
@code{mekf_mag_dip_sigma_deg}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{1.0}
 @tab Uncertainty in degrees of the magnetic reference's @strong{dip}
angle. Applies only to 3-D vector fusion (@code{mag_yaw_only = false}),
where the dip constrains roll and pitch --- a wrong dip reference
therefore biases them, permanently, and the error cannot be learned out
at sea. It is admitted into the covariance instead, as a rank-1
anisotropic term in the magnetometer noise. Set @code{0} when a position
source supplies WMM field invariants, which removes the error at its
source and is strictly better. Raising it improves covariance
consistency and lowers the @code{nis_mag} wire field by design.
@item 
@code{mag_reject_gauss}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.05}
 @tab Strong-anomaly cutoff: reject a magnetometer measurement whose
post-calibration residual exceeds this value (Gauss). Guards against
nearby iron/magnets. Fine-grained consistency is handled internally by
χ² innovation gates that self-scale with filter confidence, so keep this
a coarse threshold (~10% of the Earth field).
@item 
@code{accel_skip_thresh}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.05}
 @tab Skip an accelerometer update if `
@item 
@code{mag_yaw_only}
 @tab bool
 @tab @code{true}
 @tab Heading-only magnetometer fusion (marine default): the mag
corrects heading and never pulls on roll/pitch. The swing-circle
calibration is structurally 2D, so the field's vertical (dip) channel is
its least-calibrated component. Set @code{false} for full 3D vector
fusion --- appropriate only for magnetically clean installs with a true
3D calibration.
@item 
@code{heave_tau_s}
 @tab float
 @tab @code{12.0}
 @tab Heave filter time constant in seconds. Heave (vertical
displacement) is a band-passed double integration of vertical
acceleration; it feeds the @code{$PASHR} heave field and the binary
packet's @code{heave_m}. The passband covers ~2--15 s wave periods at
the default; allow ~2 minutes of settling after startup. @code{0}
disables (heave reads 0.0). Values far above the default are not useful
--- the passband stops covering waves --- and were once actively
dangerous: the filter's leak is sized by @code{dt/heave_tau_s}, and in
single precision it stopped working entirely at a high ODR, turning a
bounded band-pass into an unbounded double integrator (253 m of "heave"
at 32 kHz with a 900 s constant). The accumulators are double precision
as of this release, so the whole range now degrades gracefully; see
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/docs/math.md,math.md
§1}.
@item 
@code{wave_tau_s}
 @tab float
 @tab @code{120.0}
 @tab Sea-state averaging window in seconds. Significant wave height (Hs
= 4·σ(heave)), mean zero-crossing wave period, and the vessel's
roll/pitch periods and significant single amplitudes (2σ) are
exponentially weighted statistics of the heave/roll/pitch oscillations
over this window (packet fields @code{wave_height_m},
@code{wave_period_s}, @code{roll_period_s}, @code{roll_amplitude},
@code{pitch_period_s}, @code{pitch_amplitude}, gated by the
@code{wave_valid} flag). Stats settle ~2 windows after heave settles.
Oceanographic practice is 10--20 minute records, and windows that long
are supported --- but note they interact with @code{[imu] odr_hz},
because the statistics' gain is @code{dt/wave_tau_s}. At 32 kHz with a
1200 s window that gain is 2.6e-8, which single precision cannot
accumulate; significant wave height under-read by 18 % while still
reporting @code{wave_valid}. Fixed by double-precision accumulators as
of this release. Requires @code{heave_tau_s} > 0; @code{0} disables.
@item 
@code{engine_vibration_g2}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.0}
 @tab EMA threshold (m²/s⁴) for engine-vibration detection. The filter
tracks an exponential moving average of `(
@item 
@code{engine_accel_skip_thresh}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{0.20}
 @tab Accelerometer skip threshold applied while engine vibration is
detected: wider than @code{accel_skip_thresh} so the filter isn't
starved, while the accel noise is inflated ×4 so the
vibration-contaminated samples that pass are trusted proportionally
less. Only active when @code{engine_vibration_g2 > 0}.
@end multitable

@node [calibration]
@section [calibration]
@anchor{#calibration}
Calibration file and startup behaviour. @strong{[restart]}

@multitable @columnfractions 0.27 0.09 0.29 0.35 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{file}
 @tab string
 @tab @code{"/etc/imud/cal.json"}
 @tab Path to the calibration JSON produced by @code{imud-cal}. Supports
@code{~/} expansion. If the file does not exist, imud runs uncalibrated
(magnetometer readings will be biased).
@item 
@code{startup_settle_sec}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{5.0}
 @tab Seconds of sensor data to discard after chip initialisation. The
ISM330DHCX gyro drifts ~0.02 rad/s for ~5 s after power-on; discarding
this window keeps it out of the bias estimate. Set @code{0} for the
@code{sim} driver.
@item 
@code{align_window_sec}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{5.0}
 @tab Seconds of accelerometer and magnetometer averaging used for the
one-shot initial attitude alignment. Whatever tilt error survives this
window is baked permanently into the magnetic reference's dip, so in a
seaway the length matters: 1 s (the value hardcoded before 1.7) is about
a fifth of a roll period and aligns to an arbitrary point in the cycle
--- measured attitude RMS in the marine default is 47.7° at 1 s against
2.19° at 5 s, flat beyond. 3-D vector fusion keeps improving out to ~15
s. The only cost of a longer window is startup latency before usable
attitude, so raise it when starting from a mooring, not underway.
@item 
@code{gyro_bias_sec}
 @tab double
 @tab @code{2.0}
 @tab Length of the stationary window used to estimate gyro bias at
startup. The board must be still for this duration. Skipped if the cal
file already has a gyro bias. If motion is detected during the window
(gyro std > 0.5 °/s) the window is doubled once and a warning is logged.
Set @code{0} to skip bias estimation.
@end multitable

