@node [mount]
@section [mount]
@anchor{#mount}
Board-to-body rotation for installations where the chip X axis does not
point along the platform's forward axis (the bow, on a vessel).
@strong{[restart]}

The rotation is ZYX intrinsic Euler angles:
@code{R = Rz(yaw) × Ry(pitch) × Rx(roll)}. In practice only @code{yaw}
is non-zero --- it corrects for the angle between the chip X axis and
the platform's forward direction.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.27 0.14 0.23 0.36 
@headitem 
Key
 @tab Type
 @tab Default
 @tab Description
@item 
@code{rotation_euler_deg}
 @tab array
 @tab @code{[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]}
 @tab @code{[roll, pitch, yaw]} in degrees. Measure the angle between
chip X and the forward axis once (e.g. with a handbearing compass on a
vessel); set that as @code{yaw}.
@item 
@code{preset}
 @tab string
 @tab @emph{(unset)}
 @tab Named shortcut: @code{"identity"}, @code{"yaw_90"},
@code{"yaw_180"}, @code{"yaw_270"}, @code{"roll_90"}, @code{"roll_270"},
@code{"pitch_90"}, @code{"pitch_270"}. Overrides
@code{rotation_euler_deg} when set. An unrecognised name is a fatal
config error.
@item 
@code{rotation_matrix}
 @tab array of 9
 @tab @emph{(unset)}
 @tab Board→body rotation given directly, row-major
(@code{v_body = R · v_board}). Validated at load against @code{RᵀR = I}
and @code{det(R) = +1}; a non-orthonormal matrix or a reflection is a
fatal config error. Whichever mount key appears last wins.
@end multitable

@strong{Example} --- chip X points aft (180° from forward; e.g. to a
vessel's stern):

@verbatim
rotation_euler_deg = [0.0, 0.0, 180.0]
# or equivalently:
preset = "yaw_180"
@end verbatim

@node [logging]
@section [logging]
@anchor{#logging}
Diagnostic log output. @strong{[hot]}: @code{level}, @code{stats_hz}.
@strong{[restart]}: @code{file} (path changes; the file itself is
reopened on every reload).

Repeated identical messages are suppressed and logged as a
@code{last message repeated N times} count. The most recent
warnings/errors are also shown by @code{imud-status} ("Recent
warnings").

The @code{[stats]} line ends with a sample-latency clause ---
@code{fifo=p50/p99 pipe=p50/p99 max=fifo/pipe} in milliseconds --- on
parts that have a hardware timestamp counter. @code{fifo} is the age of
a sample when its read completed; @code{pipe} is the daemon's own cost
from there to fused state, and is the term to hold to a budget (0.26 ms
p99 on a Pi 5 at 833 Hz).

Three things about reading it:

@itemize
@item
@strong{The percentiles are bucket upper edges, and @code{max=} is
exact.} The histogram is log₂-spaced, so a reported @code{p99} of
@code{16.4 ms} means the true value is somewhere in @code{[8.2, 16.4)}.
It never flatters, but it can overstate by up to 2×. When you want a
number rather than a bound, use @code{max=}.
@item
@strong{@code{fifo} is set by @code{fifo_wm}.} The reader wakes on the
watermark interrupt, with a fallback of @code{fifo_wm + int_grace}
sample periods if the line is late. The fallback is by construction
later than the watermark it backs up, so on a healthy line the watermark
decides and residence is about @code{fifo_wm / odr_hz} --- measured at
75.8 ms against a predicted 76.8 ms at 833 Hz with @code{wm = 64}. See
the @code{fifo_wm} entry above.
@item
@strong{It takes a few seconds to appear}, because the chip's real tick
period has to be measured against the host clock first. On
@code{icm20948} and @code{mpu925x}, which have no chip timer, it is
absent entirely --- both are normal rather than faults.
@end itemize

A drain clause follows it --- @code{drains=E/T e/t n=MEAN max=DEEPEST}
--- saying how the FIFO is actually being emptied. @code{E} is drains
woken by the watermark interrupt, @code{T} drains woken by the fallback
timer, @code{n} the mean samples per drain and @code{max} the deepest
single burst since start.

Read the @strong{split}, not the mean: it is a health check on the
interrupt line. @code{E} should dominate overwhelmingly --- a healthy
line gives one timeout per run or none at all, because the fallback only
expires when an edge is genuinely late. A @code{T} that is climbing
means the watermark interrupt is not arriving, and the reader is running
on its fallback instead; residence then stretches to
@code{fifo_wm + int_grace} sample periods rather than @code{fifo_wm},
and the first thing to check is the wiring and @code{int_gpio}. On an
install with no IMU interrupt line, @code{E} is 0 by construction and
the cadence is the poll instead.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.14 0.09 0.12 0.65 
