@node Sample rates and which ones your host can actually run
@section Sample rates, and which ones your host can actually run
@anchor{#sample-rates-and-which-ones-your-host-can-actually-run}
Each driver advertises exactly the rates its part can be programmed to.
Ask for something else and imud rounds @strong{up} to the next one the
chip supports, tunes the filter for that rate, and logs
@code{requested, N Hz actual} at startup.

Two parts advertise rates a Raspberry Pi will not survive:

@itemize
@item
@strong{@code{icm42688p} at 16000 and 32000 Hz.} The silicon does them
(low-noise mode, which is how imud configures it), and imud is not a
Pi-only daemon --- on a host with the headroom they are usable. On a Pi
they are not. At 32 kHz the 256-sample IMU ring fills in 8 ms and the
fusion thread is asked for 32000 MEKF predictions a second; the
realistic outcome is FIFO overflow rather than data. Treat them as
available for larger hosts, not as a setting to try because it is the
biggest number.
@item
@strong{@code{ism330dhcx} / @code{lsm6dso} at 6664 Hz.} Comfortable on a
Pi 4 or 5, and the rate the SPI transport exists to reach --- I²C at 400
kHz cannot carry a 6664 Hz FIFO drain.
@end itemize

For sea-state and heave work none of this is needed: those estimators
track 0.05--1 Hz wave physics, and the sweep tests measure their
accuracy as essentially flat from 100 Hz to 16 kHz. High rates buy
vibration rejection and attitude bandwidth, not wave accuracy. The
default of 833 Hz is the right answer for almost every installation.

Two drivers deliberately advertise less than their part can do:

@itemize
@item
@strong{@code{lis3mdl}} stops at 155 Hz. Its 300, 560 and 1000 Hz modes
require dropping the XY/Z operating mode below ultra-high-performance,
which costs magnetometer noise performance --- a bad trade when the
MEKF's magnetometer update only needs heading, and 155 Hz is already far
above what that needs. (Its 0.625 Hz bottom rung is also omitted: the
rate table is integer Hz.)
@item
@strong{@code{icm20948} and @code{mpu925x}} derive their rate from an
integer divider (@code{1125/(1+div)} and @code{1000/(1+div)}), so they
reach rates no table could list. Their advertised entries are a readable
sample, not the whole set; both implement the @code{actual_odr_hz} hook
and report what they really programmed.
@end itemize

GPIO pins shown are the defaults (@code{imu.int_gpio = 17},
@code{mag.int_gpio = 27}). Set @code{int_gpio = 0} to disable the
interrupt and use a polling timer --- useful when the pin is wired
differently or unavailable.

@strong{Experimental} drivers have their register maps verified against
the datasheet but have @strong{not} been validated on physical hardware.
imud prints a warning at startup when an experimental driver is
selected. To add a new driver, see
@ref{11 Writing a driver,,§11 Writing a driver}.

If you have one of these parts, you can clear its experimental flag for
everyone: run @code{imud-imutest} (@code{man 8 imud-imutest}, shipped in
@code{imud-utils}) against it and open an issue with the report it
writes.

@node Fitting an MPU-9250 or MPU-9255
@section Fitting an MPU-9250 or MPU-9255
@anchor{#fitting-an-mpu-9250-or-mpu-9255}
Wiring, the @code{config.txt} I²C settings, and a ready-made
@code{imud.conf} follow.

Three things about this part differ from imud's shipped defaults, and
all three are worth knowing before you wire one up.

@itemize
@item
@strong{The FIFO is smaller than the default watermark.} 512 bytes ÷ 12
bytes per accel+gyro sample-set is about 42 sets, and @code{imu.fifo_wm}
defaults to @code{64}, which this chip cannot reach. The driver logs the
mismatch at startup and drains whatever is pending instead. Roughly 42
ms of headroom at 1 kHz also means a stalled reader raises
@code{FLAG_FIFO_OVERFLOW} far sooner than on the ISM330DHCX's much
larger FIFO.
@item
@strong{The default ODR is not on the grid.} Output rate is
@code{1000 / (1 + SMPLRT_DIV)}, giving 1000 / 500 / 333 / 250 / 200 /
125 / 100 Hz. imud's default of 833 Hz is not reachable and rounds to
1000 Hz, which also costs more CPU on a Pi. Set @code{imu.odr_hz}
explicitly.
@item
@strong{Do not copy the datasheet noise figure into
@code{mekf_accel_noise}.} The MPU-9250's accelerometer noise density
(~300 µg/√Hz) converts naively to about @code{0.0035}, which lands
@emph{inside} the divergence region documented in
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/docs/math.md,math.md
§4.7} and @code{man 5 imud.conf}. @code{mekf_accel_noise} is a tuned
filter parameter, not a per-chip datasheet transcription. A noisier IMU
is not a reason to change it.
@end itemize

The magnetometer is a separate driver: set @code{mag.driver = "ak8963"}
and @code{mag.i2c_addr = 0x0C}. It is reached through the MPU's I²C
bypass, which the IMU driver opens during init, so the IMU must be
configured too --- imud always brings the IMU up first.

Boards sold as MPU-9250 are very often relabelled @strong{MPU-6500s},
which have no magnetometer at all. @code{probe()} rejects those by name
rather than letting the failure surface later as an unexplained I²C
error from the mag driver.

@node Running a sensor on SPI
@section Running a sensor on SPI
@anchor{#running-a-sensor-on-spi}
SPI moves the same registers over a faster link. That matters in two
places: the high sample rates a part advertises stop being limited by
the bus, and the FIFO drain --- which @code{imud-mon} reports separately
from the daemon's own pipeline latency --- gets shorter. An ISM330DHCX
FIFO word costs roughly 180 µs of bus time at 400 kHz I²C and under 10
µs at 10 MHz SPI.

Enable the bus (@code{dtparam=spi=on}, or @code{raspi-config} →
Interface Options → SPI), then wire each sensor to its own chip select
and name the node:

@verbatim
[imu]
driver       = "ism330dhcx"
bus          = "spi"
spi_dev      = "/dev/spidev0.0"   # CE0, header pin 24
int_gpio     = 17                 # unchanged — the interrupt is a separate wire

[mag]
driver       = "mmc5983ma"
bus          = "spi"
spi_dev      = "/dev/spidev0.1"   # CE1, header pin 26
int_gpio     = 27
@end verbatim

Points worth knowing:

@itemize
@item
@strong{The chip select does the addressing.} @code{i2c_addr} is unused
on SPI, and the two sensors need @emph{separate} chip selects --- they
cannot share one node.
@item
@strong{The sections are independent.} A SPI IMU with an I²C compass is
a legal and sometimes necessary rig: the AKM compasses have no SPI port,
so a 9-axis ICM-20948 or MPU-925x board stays on I²C entirely.
@item
@strong{@code{spi_speed_hz = 0} means the part's datasheet maximum},
which is usually what you want. A higher request is clamped and the
daemon logs the rate it really programmed.
@item
@strong{The interrupt line is unchanged.} @code{int_gpio} is a separate
wire either way; only the data path moves.
@item
@strong{Wiring is not modelled by any test.} The tests prove both
transports produce identical register traffic, which is a different
claim from "the board is wired right". Run @code{imud-imutest --all}
after changing transport --- its report should match the one you get on
I²C.
@end itemize

