@node 6 Calibration
@chapter 6. Calibration
@anchor{#6-calibration}
Calibration is strongly recommended before first use. Stop the daemon
first (@code{imud-cal} talks to the hardware directly):

@verbatim
sudo systemctl stop imud
@end verbatim

@code{imud-cal [--config PATH] [--output PATH] <mode>} writes to the
path set by @code{calibration.file} (default @code{/etc/imud/cal.json}),
or to @code{--output PATH}. A partial run updates only the section it
calibrated, preserving the others.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.19 0.81 
@headitem 
Mode
 @tab Procedure
@item 
@code{gyro}
 @tab Hold the board completely still. Captures gyro bias over a short
window.
@item 
@code{accel}
 @tab Bench 6-position calibration; follow the on-screen prompts to
orient each face in turn (see below). Do this before final mounting.
@item 
@code{mag}
 @tab In-situ magnetometer swing --- drive the vessel slowly through at
least two full 360° circles, then press Ctrl-C. Guided: a live bar shows
heading-circle coverage (@code{#} covered, @code{.} needed, @code{o} =
where you're pointing), running coverage %, and live radius/RMS; a bell
+ "FULL CIRCLE" message tells you when coverage is complete. Must be
done after final mounting, with the engine and typical electronics
running.
@item 
@code{characterize}
 @tab Offline Allan-variance noise analysis of a @strong{stationary}
capture (@code{--from FILE}, recorded with @code{[capture]} enabled ---
overnight for a trustworthy bias-instability floor). Writes per-axis
gyro/accel noise characteristics to cal.json's @code{noise} section as
@strong{informational} sensor characterization --- the filter always
keeps its tuned @code{mekf_*}; these numbers never feed it. Never
touches the sensors.
@item 
@code{fit-temp}
 @tab Offline linear gyro-bias/temperature fit from a warm-up capture
(@code{--from FILE}, cold boot → warm, several °C of span). The daemon
subtracts the fitted term from the gyro before fusion. Never touches the
sensors.
@item 
@code{fit-ra}
 @tab Offline check of the filter's accelerometer measurement model
against a @strong{rough-water} capture (@code{--from FILE}). Reports the
gravity-direction residual, its correlation time, the
innovation-distance distribution against the gates, and the mean NIS ---
the same statistic the daemon publishes live as @code{nis_accel}.
@strong{Writes nothing}: the value it comments on is filter tuning in
@code{imud.conf}, not a sensor property. Needs a mag calibration in
cal.json. Never touches the sensors.
@end multitable

@menu
* The six accelerometer positions::
* Checking the filter against real water::
@end menu

@node The six accelerometer positions
@section The six accelerometer positions
@anchor{#the-six-accelerometer-positions}
Remember that the board frame has @strong{Z pointing down} (§11), so the
axis pointing @strong{up} reads +g and the axis pointing @strong{down}
reads −g. Flat and component-side up therefore reads
@code{[0, 0, −9.807]}, not @code{+9.807}.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.03 0.54 0.19 0.24 
@headitem 
#
 @tab Position
 @tab Board axis
 @tab Expected reading
@item 
1
 @tab Flat, component side up
 @tab +Z points down
 @tab @code{[0, 0, −9.807]}
@item 
2
 @tab Flat, upside down
 @tab +Z points up
 @tab @code{[0, 0, +9.807]}
@item 
3
 @tab Nose down (bow edge on the bench)
 @tab +X points down
 @tab @code{[−9.807, 0, 0]}
@item 
4
 @tab Nose up (stern edge on the bench)
 @tab +X points up
 @tab @code{[+9.807, 0, 0]}
@item 
5
 @tab Starboard side down (right edge down)
 @tab +Y points down
 @tab @code{[0, −9.807, 0]}
@item 
6
 @tab Port side down (left edge down)
 @tab +Y points up
 @tab @code{[0, +9.807, 0]}
@end multitable

Each prompt states the expected vector before you take the reading, and
prints what it actually measured afterwards, so a misplaced board shows
up immediately. If a reading is geometrically impossible --- gravity
landing on an axis other than the one asked for, or a pair taken the
wrong way round --- the run names the offending position and exits
@strong{without writing anything}. A calibration fitted from a misplaced
board is worse than no calibration, so it is not offered for saving.

These are the same six orientations @code{imud-imutest} uses for its
guided axis-sign check, so the two tools can be cross-checked against
each other.

The magnetometer calibration fits a hard-iron offset and a 2D soft-iron
correction (including the cross term, so a distortion ellipse whose axes
are rotated relative to the sensor is corrected --- a per-axis scale
cannot do this). It reports the field radius, a fit residual, and swing
coverage; a residual under ~1 µT and coverage above ~75% indicate a good
fit. Afterward, watch the wire's @code{mag_residual} /
@code{mag_anomaly} compass-health metrics (v14, also on the InfluxDB and
Prometheus bridges): a rising residual means the calibration is
degrading and the swing should be repeated.

@node Checking the filter against real water
@section Checking the filter against real water
@anchor{#checking-the-filter-against-real-water}
Two knobs decide how much the filter trusts the accelerometer, and they
are not interchangeable:

@itemize
@item
@strong{@code{mekf_accel_noise}} describes the @emph{sensor's} white
noise. It is the knob most likely to be "improved" into a worse
configuration: the default is a sharp local optimum for the marine
(yaw-only) configuration, and values a little above it are substantially
@emph{worse}, not a little worse --- see @code{man 5 imud.conf}.
@item
@strong{@code{mekf_wave_accel}} (with @code{mekf_wave_accel_tau_s})
describes the @emph{seaway}. This is the one to reach for when the boat
is in rough water. It models the wave-orbital disturbance as a
correlated process in the filter state, which is what lets the filter
stop over-counting 833 nearly identical samples a second as independent
evidence about gravity.
@end itemize

If you run 3-D vector fusion (@code{mag_yaw_only = false}) there is a
third, and it is about the @emph{magnetic reference} rather than either
sensor: @code{mekf_mag_dip_sigma_deg}. In that mode the field's dip
constrains roll and pitch, and the dip of the reference is fixed once at
alignment --- so a tilt error at startup becomes a permanent roll/pitch
bias. The best fix is a position source, which supplies WMM field
invariants and removes the error outright; failing that, this key tells
the filter how much the dip channel is worth. @code{align_window_sec}
matters here too: it sets how much of the wave cycle is averaged out
before the reference is fixed.

Before changing either, measure:

@enumerate 
@item
@strong{Record a capture in the conditions you care about.} Enable
@code{[capture]} and run for at least 20 minutes in the seaway you want
the filter tuned for. The capture stores raw pre-calibration samples, so
one recording can be re-analysed against any tuning.

@item
@strong{Analyse it offline} --- this touches nothing and can run on any
machine:

@verbatim
imud-cal fit-ra --from /var/lib/imud/<capture>.imucap --config /etc/imud/imud.conf
@end verbatim

@item
@strong{Read the mean NIS.} With the wave state enabled (the default)
this @strong{should sit near 1}: the correlated part of the residual is
modelled rather than left for a white R to absorb. Well above 1 means
the seaway is rougher than @code{mekf_wave_accel} allows for; well below
1 means calmer, which is safe but conservative. (With the wave state
@emph{disabled}, 10--30 is normal and is not by itself a reason to
change anything --- that reading is the problem the wave state exists to
solve.)

@item
@strong{Read the wave-state block.} @code{fit-ra} replays the capture a
second time with the state forced off, so it reports the disturbance the
filter is actually up against --- σ in m/s² and its correlation time ---
and suggests values for both knobs. Round them up.

@item
@strong{Check the residual @emph{mean}, not just its spread.} A large
per-axis mean points at a wrong mount rotation or a stale accelerometer
calibration --- neither of which any noise setting can fix.

@end enumerate

Live, the same statistic is on the wire as @code{nis_accel} (and on the
Prometheus and InfluxDB bridges as @code{imud_nis_accel_ratio} /
@code{nis_accel}), alongside @code{innov_weight} and
@code{innov_reject}, which say how hard the innovation gate is having to
work. All three of @code{mekf_accel_noise}, @code{mekf_wave_accel} and
@code{mekf_wave_accel_tau_s} are re-read on @code{SIGHUP}, so an A/B is:
edit the value, @code{systemctl reload imud}, and watch the metric
settle for a couple of minutes.

Restart when done:

@verbatim
sudo systemctl start imud
@end verbatim

