@end ifnottex

@node 7 Output streams
@chapter 7. Output streams
@anchor{#7-output-streams}
imud publishes on up to three streams simultaneously. Full wire formats
are in
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/spec.md,spec.md
§7--8, §10}.

@quotation
@strong{Default posture (since 1.6):} a stock daemon enables only the
local AF_UNIX stream socket. Every network output --- NMEA (UDP
@emph{and} TCP), high-rate UDP, the stream TCP listener --- is off until
explicitly enabled in @code{imud.conf}. Nothing touches the network out
of the box.
@end quotation

@menu
* NMEA 0183 --- UDP broadcast and/or TCP listener port 10110 default off::
* High-rate binary --- UDP port 10111 default off::
* Local subscription stream --- AF_UNIX socket default on + TCP listener default off::
* NMEA 2000 --- via Signal K recipe no imud code involved::
@end menu

@node NMEA 0183 --- UDP broadcast and/or TCP listener port 10110 default off
@section NMEA 0183 --- UDP broadcast and/or TCP listener, port 10110 (default off)
@anchor{#nmea-0183--udp-broadcast-andor-tcp-listener-port-10110-default-off}
Text sentences for chartplotters, autopilots, and marine software
(Signal K, OpenCPN). Two transports, independently enabled:

@itemize
@item
@strong{UDP broadcast} (@code{enabled}) --- for listen-only consumers on
the LAN.
@item
@strong{TCP listener} (@code{tcp_enabled}) --- most plotter apps
(OpenCPN, Navionics, iOS/Android nav apps) connect as TCP clients; point
them at @code{tcp://<host>:10110}. Up to 8 clients; a slow client skips
bursts, never stalls the daemon.
@end itemize

Per burst at @code{nmea.rate_hz}:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.12 0.88 
@headitem 
Sentence
 @tab Contents
@item 
@code{$PASHR}
 @tab Roll, pitch, heading, @strong{heave}, accuracy flags.
@item 
@code{$HCHDM}
 @tab Magnetic heading.
@item 
@code{$HCHDG}
 @tab Heading with magnetic variation (variation fields filled when
declination is known).
@item 
@code{$TIROT}
 @tab Rate of turn (deg/min).
@item 
@code{$IIXDR}
 @tab Pitch and roll as transducer measurements.
@item 
@code{$HCHDT}
 @tab True heading --- @strong{only} emitted when declination is
configured.
@end multitable

@node High-rate binary --- UDP port 10111 default off
@section High-rate binary --- UDP port 10111 (default off)
@anchor{#high-rate-binary--udp-port-10111-default-off}
A fixed 276-byte little-endian packet (wire v17) at up to 500 Hz:
calibrated and raw accel/gyro/mag, quaternion, Euler angles, heading,
rate-of-turn, heave, temperature, the 3×3 attitude covariance,
timestamps (wall + TAI + chip), declination, and an IEEE-802.3 CRC32.
Every packet is self-describing (magic + version + CRC), so consumers
can validate each one independently. See
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/spec.md,spec.md
§8} for the exact layout and the consumer libraries in
@ref{9 Consumer libraries,,§9}.

@strong{Near-vertical pitch --- read the quaternion.} The Euler fields
(@code{pitch}, @code{roll}, @code{yaw}, @code{heading_deg},
@code{rate_of_turn}) are derived from the quaternion as ZYX intrinsic
angles, which are singular at pitch = ±90°: approaching vertical, roll
and yaw stop being separately determined and swing wildly, and at ±90°
they are undefined. That is a property of any three-angle
representation, not a filter limitation. The quaternion is never
singular. Surface vessels never go near this and can use the Euler
fields freely; a gimbal, tilting camera rig, or anything that can point
straight up or down must read @code{quat_*} instead. Same for the NMEA
sentences above, which are Euler by definition.

@node Local subscription stream --- AF_UNIX socket default on + TCP listener default off
@section Local subscription stream --- AF_UNIX socket (default on) + TCP listener (default off)
@anchor{#local-subscription-stream--af_unix-socket-default-on--tcp-listener-default-off}
@quotation
This is the one output a stock daemon enables --- the bridges and
libimud consumers read it.
@end quotation
The same 276-byte binary packets over a @code{SOCK_STREAM} socket at
@code{/run/imud/imud-stream.sock}. Same-host consumers connect and
receive a loss-free stream (no datagram drops). Ideal for co-located
machine-vision or gimbal processes. Up to 8 subscribers; a consumer that
can't keep up gets packet gaps (visible in @code{imu_seq}), never blocks
the daemon.

With @code{[stream] tcp_enabled = true} the same framed packets are also
served on a TCP listener (default port 10112) --- the payoff is
@strong{remote consumers}: run @code{imud-mon}, the Python client
(@code{ImudClient.connect_tcp("boat.local")}), or any libimud program
(@code{imud_connect_tcp}, see @code{libimud(3)}) on a laptop against a
daemon elsewhere on the network, with the same lossless framing as the
local socket. Both listeners are fed by one thread at @code{rate_hz}.

@strong{If that thread cannot be started, imud exits 1} rather than
running without it --- a daemon reporting active while producing nothing
at all is worse than one that is plainly down, and @code{imud.service}
is @code{Restart=on-failure} with @code{RestartSec=3}. The optional
outputs (@code{[nmea]}, @code{[highrate]}, and the position thread) are
the other way round: they warn and the daemon carries on. In that case
whatever they had already bound is closed and unlinked, so a client gets
@code{ECONNREFUSED} immediately instead of connecting successfully to a
listener nobody is serving and then waiting forever. @code{READY=1} is
sent to systemd only once the threads are actually running, not when the
sockets are bound.

@node NMEA 2000 --- via Signal K recipe no imud code involved
@section NMEA 2000 --- via Signal K (recipe, no imud code involved)
@anchor{#nmea-2000--via-signal-k-recipe-no-imud-code-involved}
imud has no direct N2K output by design: the Signal K server already
owns the hard device-level work (ISO address claim, product info) and
its
@uref{https://github.com/SignalK/signalk-to-nmea2000,@code{signalk-to-nmea2000}}
plugin converts the paths imud-signalk emits into PGNs. The chain is:

@verbatim
imud → imud-signalk (UDP delta) → Signal K server → signalk-to-nmea2000 → CAN bus
@end verbatim

@strong{You need:} a CAN interface the server can transmit on --- a
SocketCAN hat (PiCAN2, Waveshare MCP2515, @dots{}) using a
@code{canbus (canboatjs)} connection, or an Actisense NGT-1
(@code{actisense-serial} with @code{toChildProcess: nmea2000out}).

@strong{Steps:}

@enumerate 
@item
Enable imud's stream + a bridge output: @code{imud.conf}
@code{[stream] enabled = true}; @code{imud-signalk.conf}
@code{udp_enabled = true} (the daemon is enabled by default),
destination = the Signal K host, port 10113 (default).
@code{sudo systemctl enable --now imud-signalk}.
@item
Signal K server → @emph{Data Connections} → add: data type
@strong{SignalK}, source @strong{UDP}, port @strong{10113}. imud's paths
now appear under @code{vessels.self}.
@item
Add the N2K connection (canbus/canboatjs or Actisense) with output
enabled, install @code{signalk-to-nmea2000} from the Appstore, and
enable the conversions below in its plugin config.
@end enumerate

@strong{Verified conversions} (checked against the plugin source; each
is a separate toggle):

@multitable @columnfractions 0.09 0.33 0.33 0.25 
@headitem 
PGN
 @tab Content
 @tab Plugin conversion
 @tab Feeds from
@item 
127250
 @tab Vessel heading (magnetic + variation)
 @tab @code{Heading (127250)}
 @tab @code{navigation.headingMagnetic} +
@code{navigation.magneticVariation}
@item 
127250
 @tab Vessel heading (true)
 @tab @code{TrueHeading (127250)}
 @tab @code{navigation.headingTrue}
@item 
127257
 @tab Attitude (roll/pitch/yaw)
 @tab @code{Attitude (127257)}
 @tab @code{navigation.attitude}
@item 
127258
 @tab Magnetic variation
 @tab @code{Magnetic Variation (127258)}
 @tab @code{navigation.magneticVariation}
@end multitable

Enable imud's declination (WMM via @code{[position]}, or a static value)
--- the magnetic-heading conversion takes variation as an input, and the
true-heading and variation conversions have nothing to emit without it.

@strong{Not covered by the official plugin:} rate of turn (PGN 127251)
and heave (127252) have no conversions there --- imud emits
@code{navigation.rateOfTurn}, but nothing converts it. If you need
127251 on the backbone, the community fork
@uref{https://github.com/NearlCrews/signalk-nmea2000-emitter-cannon,@code{signalk-nmea2000-emitter-cannon}}
advertises broader PGN coverage (unverified here), and most N2K
autopilots and displays derive turn rate from the heading PGN
themselves.

