@node 8 Monitoring and diagnostics
@chapter 8. Monitoring and diagnostics
@anchor{#8-monitoring-and-diagnostics}

@menu
* imud-status::
* imud-mon::
* imud-imutest::
* Raw capture with netcat::
* Logging::
@end menu

@node imud-status
@section imud-status
@anchor{#imud-status}
Query a running daemon over its Unix socket:

@verbatim
imud-status
@end verbatim

It prints chip IDs, ODRs, fusion convergence and covariance, calibration
flags, current attitude, declination and true heading, heave, per-stream
output status, sample counts and overflow counts, uptime, and a "Recent
warnings" section listing the last few warning/error log lines. Use
@code{--socket PATH} for a non-default socket.

@node imud-mon
@section imud-mon
@anchor{#imud-mon}
A receive-side monitor that listens on the UDP output ports from any
host on the LAN (it needs no access to the daemon's socket):

@verbatim
imud-mon                      # both UDP streams, one line each, once/sec
imud-mon nmea                 # only the named stream
imud-mon binary
imud-mon --config config/sim.conf   # read ports/addresses from a specific config
@end verbatim

It reads the port numbers and multicast addresses from the config file
and validates each binary packet (magic + CRC) before display.

@node imud-imutest
@section imud-imutest
@anchor{#imud-imutest}
Validates a sensor driver against real hardware and writes a Markdown
report suitable for attaching to an issue. Ships in @code{imud-utils};
full documentation is in @code{man 8 imud-imutest} and under
@code{/usr/share/doc/imud-utils/}.

@verbatim
sudo systemctl stop imud       # both would drain the same FIFO
imud-imutest --imu-driver icm20948 --mag-driver ak09916 --mag-addr 0x0C --all
sudo systemctl start imud
@end verbatim

It runs a fully automatic pass (probe, reset timing, register readback,
measured ODR, FIFO depth and overflow, @code{seq} continuity, the
error-return contract, noise, gravity, temperature, @code{chip_ts},
interrupt edges, full-scale sweep) and then three guided phases that
prompt the operator to place and turn the board --- the only way to
prove the chip-to-board axis remap is right.

This is how an experimental driver gets its flag cleared: run it, then
open an issue with the report.

@node Raw capture with netcat
@section Raw capture with netcat
@anchor{#raw-capture-with-netcat}

@verbatim
nc -u -l 10110                    # watch NMEA sentences (UDP broadcast)
nc -u -l 10111 > raw_packets.bin  # capture raw binary packets
nc HOST 10110                     # NMEA over TCP ([nmea] tcp_enabled)
nc HOST 10112 | xxd | head        # framed binary stream over TCP
                                  #   ([stream] tcp_enabled)
@end verbatim

@node Logging
@section Logging
@anchor{#logging-1}
imud logs to stderr (captured by journald under systemd) or to a file
set by @code{logging.file}. The verbosity is @code{logging.level} and is
hot-reloadable. Repeated identical messages are collapsed into a repeat
count, and under systemd each line carries an sd-daemon priority so
@code{journalctl -p warning} works. For a file log, rotate it with
logrotate and a @code{postrotate: systemctl reload imud} (the file is
reopened on SIGHUP). See the @ref{[logging],,[logging]} config section.

