@node 10 Troubleshooting
@chapter 10. Troubleshooting
@anchor{#10-troubleshooting}
@multitable @columnfractions 0.34 0.66 
@headitem 
Symptom
 @tab Check
@item 
Sensors not detected (I²C)
 @tab I²C enabled? @code{i2cdetect -y 1} should list @code{0x6b}
(ISM330DHCX) and @code{0x30} (MMC5983MA). Check wiring and the
@code{i2c_addr} values.
@item 
Sensors not detected (SPI)
 @tab SPI enabled (@code{dtparam=spi=on}) and @code{/dev/spidev0.*}
present? Check @code{spi_dev} names the right chip select, that each
sensor has its @strong{own} CE line, and that the driver supports SPI at
all --- see @ref{5 Supported drivers,,Supported drivers}. A
@code{probe()} failure here is usually MISO/MOSI swapped or the wrong
CE.
@item 
@code{WHO_AM_I} mismatch at startup
 @tab Wrong @code{imu.driver}/@code{mag.driver}, or a wrong
@code{i2c_addr} (e.g. SA0 jumper → 0x6A vs 0x6B). On SPI the address
comes from the chip select, so suspect @code{spi_dev} instead.
@item 
GPIO open/permission errors
 @tab Run @code{gpiodetect} and set @code{gpio_chip} to the chip it
lists for the header pins --- the number comes from probe order, so it
is not stable across kernels (Pi 5 moved from @code{gpiochip4} to
@code{gpiochip0} in mid-2024). Run as the @code{imud} service user or a
member of the @code{gpio}/@code{i2c}/@code{spi} groups.
@item 
@code{/dev/gpiochip4} vanished after an upgrade, on a Pi 5
 @tab Pi OS aliases the header chip to its old name with a udev rule
guarded by @code{test ! -c /dev/gpiochip4}. That guard oscillates: any
@code{udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=gpio} deletes the symlink when
it exists and recreates it when it does not. Re-trigger, or reboot, to
get it back --- and prefer @code{gpiochip0}, which is the real device.
imud stopped triggering the @code{gpio} subsystem in 1.9.0 for exactly
this reason.
@item 
Bus or GPIO open fails only under systemd, but works when run by hand
 @tab The unit's @code{DevicePolicy=closed} allows a fixed list of
nodes. The shipped list covers @code{/dev/i2c-1}, @code{/dev/i2c-3},
@code{/dev/spidev0.0}, @code{/dev/spidev0.1}, @code{/dev/gpiochip0} and
@code{/dev/gpiochip4}; anything else --- SPI1..6, for instance --- needs
its own @code{DeviceAllow=} line. @code{systemd-analyze verify} will not
catch this --- it only bites at device-open time.
@item 
Filter reports @code{R} in @code{imud-mon}, or @code{imud_state_reset}
is 1
 @tab The MEKF found a non-finite value in its own state and reset
itself; it re-aligns automatically and the flag clears when it
re-converges. Repeated resets are a bug --- capture the log line
(@code{[fusion] non-finite filter state}) and the @code{.imucap} that
produced it.
@item 
Fusion never converges
 @tab Magnetometer uncalibrated, or a strong local magnetic disturbance.
Run @code{imud-cal mag}; check the fit residual.
@item 
Heading drifts steadily while pitch and roll stay put
 @tab The magnetometer is uncalibrated, so every mag sample is marked
invalid, the filter's mag update never runs and heading is dead-reckoned
from the gyro --- measured on a static bench, 220° in 24 minutes while
pitch and roll held to a tenth of a degree. @code{imud-status} says so
explicitly under Attitude; the fix is @code{imud-cal mag}.
@item 
Heading stopped updating altogether
 @tab The magnetometer has stopped delivering. The daemon warns once per
outage (@code{[mag_reader] no magnetometer sample for @dots{}}) and logs
the recovery. On a shared SPI controller, check
@code{[imu] spi_speed_hz} is not below 2.5@ MHz.
@item 
Heading is off by a constant
 @tab Mount rotation. Set @code{mount.rotation_euler_deg} yaw to the
chip-X-to-bow angle.
@item 
No true heading output
 @tab Declination not configured. Set
@code{position.lat_deg}/@code{lon_deg}, or @code{declination_deg}, or
enable gpsd/SignalK.
@item 
No NMEA received
 @tab @code{nmea.enabled = true} (UDP) or @code{nmea.tcp_enabled = true}
(TCP)? Both default @strong{off} since 1.6. Consumer listening on /
connecting to the right port? Broadcast reachable on the subnet?
@item 
Config change ignored
 @tab @code{[restart]} keys need a full restart, not a @code{reload}.
Check the log for a parse error (a bad value keeps the old config on
reload, and prevents startup on boot).
@end multitable

@code{imud-status} and the daemon log (@code{journalctl -u imud}) are
the first place to look. The expected first-boot log sequence and common
failure modes are in
@uref{https://github.com/richcreations/imud/blob/main/spec.md,spec.md
§11}.

