@node 11 Writing a driver
@chapter 11. Writing a driver
@anchor{#11-writing-a-driver}
This section explains how to add support for a new IMU or magnetometer
chip. The driver abstraction lives in @code{include/drivers.h}; existing
drivers in @code{src/drivers/} are the best reference. Start by reading
@code{src/drivers/sim.c} (the simulation driver) --- it is the minimal
viable implementation of both interfaces and has no hardware
dependencies.

@menu
* Overview::
* Conventions that must be followed exactly::
* The register helpers::
* Writing an IMU driver imu_ops_t::
* Writing a magnetometer driver mag_ops_t::
* The supported_* tables::
* The actual_odr_hz hook::
* Registering the driver::
* Testing without hardware --- the sim driver::
* Pre-submission checklist::
@end menu

@node Overview
@section Overview
@anchor{#overview}
imud separates chips into two independent driver types:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.38 0.33 0.28 
@headitem 
Type
 @tab Interface
 @tab Example chips
@item 
@strong{IMU} (@code{imu_ops_t})
 @tab Accelerometer + gyroscope
 @tab ISM330DHCX, ICM-20948
@item 
@strong{Magnetometer} (@code{mag_ops_t})
 @tab 3-axis magnetic field
 @tab MMC5983MA, AK09916
@end multitable

Each type is a struct of function pointers plus a few capability flags
and supported-rate tables. The daemon calls through these pointers and
never touches chip-specific registers directly.

Both types are handed an @code{imud_bus_t} --- a transport handle ---
and reach the chip through the helpers in @code{src/drivers/bus_io.h}:

@verbatim
int bus_reg_read  (const imud_bus_t *bus, uint8_t reg, uint8_t *val);
int bus_reg_write (const imud_bus_t *bus, uint8_t reg, uint8_t val);
int bus_burst_read(const imud_bus_t *bus, uint8_t reg, uint8_t *buf, uint16_t len);
@end verbatim

The handle carries the descriptor, the transport, and whatever addresses
the part --- the 7-bit slave address on I²C, the resolved clock and
framing bits on SPI. @strong{Write the register logic once; it runs on
either bus.} The helpers are @code{static inline} so every driver still
issues its own single @code{ioctl()}, which is what
@code{test/bus_mock.c} intercepts with @code{--wrap=ioctl}; do not
reroute them through @code{I2C_SLAVE}, SMBus calls, or
@code{read()}/@code{write()}.

To offer SPI, fill in @code{bus_caps} from the datasheet:

@verbatim
.bus_caps = { .spi_capable = true, .spi_mode = 3,
              .spi_max_hz = 10000000, .spi_inc_mask = 0 },
@end verbatim

Leave it zeroed and @code{bus = "spi"} is refused at startup by name ---
the right outcome for a part with no SPI port, and better than silently
mis-framing. @code{spi_inc_mask} is the one field that is easy to get
wrong: some parts step the address on a multi-byte read automatically
(the ST 6-axis parts do it from @code{CTRL3_C}'s @code{IF_INC}), and
some need an explicit bit in the command byte (LIS3MDL's MS at
@code{0x40}). Check the SPI section of the datasheet, not the I²C one
--- the auto-increment bit is frequently in a different place on each.

If a part cannot do SPI, say why in the ops struct rather than leaving
the field zeroed without comment; @code{src/drivers/lis2mdl.c} is the
worked example (its 4-wire mode disables the data-ready line the driver
depends on).

@node Conventions that must be followed exactly
@section Conventions that must be followed exactly
@anchor{#conventions-that-must-be-followed-exactly}
Getting these wrong produces silently wrong attitude output.

@menu
* Output coordinate frame --- NED-compatible board frame::
* Accelerometer output --- specific force m/s²::
* Gyroscope output --- rad/s right-hand rule::
* Magnetometer output --- µT::
* Axis remapping for the magnetometer::
@end menu

@node Output coordinate frame --- NED-compatible board frame
@subsection Output coordinate frame --- NED-compatible board frame
@anchor{#output-coordinate-frame--ned-compatible-board-frame}
All drivers must output sensor data in the @strong{NED-compatible board
frame}:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.06 0.94 
@headitem 
Axis
 @tab Direction
@item 
X
 @tab Chip X marking (typically toward bow / forward)
@item 
Y
 @tab Starboard (right when facing forward)
@item 
Z
 @tab @strong{Down} (into the PCB / toward earth when component-side up)
@end multitable

This is a right-handed frame. With this convention and
@code{rotation_euler_deg = [0, 0, 0]}, a component-up installation with
chip X pointing bow maps directly to NED with no additional rotation.
Only yaw needs setting when the chip X axis does not align with the bow.

@node Accelerometer output --- specific force m/s²
@subsection Accelerometer output --- specific force, m/s²
@anchor{#accelerometer-output--specific-force-ms²}
Drivers report @strong{specific force} in the NED-compatible board
frame.

@itemize
@item
@strong{Flat, component-side up:} reads approximately
@code{[0, 0, −9.80665]} m/s² on Z (the reaction force is upward = −Z in
this frame).
@item
@strong{Free-falling:} reads @code{[0, 0, 0]}.
@end itemize

Scale raw ADC counts to m/s² using the datasheet sensitivity (typically
mg/LSB × 9.80665 → m/s²/LSB), then apply any axis remapping needed to
reach the board frame above.

@node Gyroscope output --- rad/s right-hand rule
@subsection Gyroscope output --- rad/s, right-hand rule
@anchor{#gyroscope-output--rads-right-hand-rule}
Scale raw counts to @strong{rad/s} (not deg/s):

@verbatim
const float d2r = (float)(M_PI / 180.0 / 1000.0);  /* mdps → rad/s */
gyro_scale = sensitivity_mdps_per_lsb * d2r;
@end verbatim

Apply the same axis remapping as accel. Positive Z rotation = clockwise
from above (yaw right) in the NED Z-down convention.

@node Magnetometer output --- µT
@subsection Magnetometer output --- µT
@anchor{#magnetometer-output--µt}
Output magnetic field in @strong{microtesla (µT)} in the same board
frame as the IMU. If your datasheet gives mGauss/LSB or counts/Gauss,
convert with @code{1 Gauss = 100 µT}.

@node Axis remapping for the magnetometer
@subsection Axis remapping for the magnetometer
@anchor{#axis-remapping-for-the-magnetometer}
Many magnetometers share a PCB with the IMU but with one or more axes
physically inverted. After scaling, apply the sign flips needed so the
mag output is in the same NED-compatible board frame as the IMU.

For the SparkFun 9DoF (SEN-19895): the MMC5983MA Z is opposite the
ISM330DHCX chip-native Z (SparkFun marks this "MAG -Z"). After the ISM
driver remaps its own axes, the raw MMC Z already points down, so no Z
change is needed; the Y axis is the same physical direction as the ISM
chip-native Y (port), so it must be negated to reach starboard:

@verbatim
out->field[1] = -(ry - NULL_FIELD) * scale;   /* flip Y: port → starboard */
out->field[2] =  (rz - NULL_FIELD) * scale;   /* Z already points down */
@end verbatim

@node The register helpers
@section The register helpers
@anchor{#the-register-helpers}
All drivers share the register helpers in @code{src/drivers/bus_io.h}
--- include it and call them; do not roll your own:

@verbatim
#include "bus_io.h"

bus_burst_read(bus, reg, buf, len);   /* combined write-then-read */
bus_reg_write (bus, reg, val);
bus_reg_read  (bus, reg, &val);       /* burst_read of length 1 */
reg_s16le(p);  reg_s16be(p);          /* int16 from a register byte pair */
reg_s24be(p);                         /* int24, sign-extended (RM3100) */
@end verbatim

They take the @code{imud_bus_t *} the daemon handed your op, not a
descriptor and an address: the handle carries the transport, the slave
address and the SPI framing, which is what lets one body of register
logic run on either bus. @code{bus_burst_read} dispatches to a combined
write-then-read in a single I²C transaction (no repeated-start gap, ~40
µs saved against two transactions at 400 kHz), or to a command byte plus
data inside one @code{SPI_IOC_MESSAGE} so chip select stays asserted
across both.

If your chip needs a sub-address modifier and only on one transport,
name the transport rather than ORing the bit unconditionally ---
@code{src/drivers/lis3mdl.c} is the worked example:

@verbatim
uint8_t out_reg = REG_OUT_X_L;
if (bus->kind == BUS_I2C) out_reg |= 0x80;   /* I²C auto-increment */
@end verbatim

The SPI half of that is not a call-site decision at all: it rides in the
handle as @code{spi_inc_mask}, from the driver's @code{bus_caps}. Beware
datasheets that tabulate a "read address" --- on some parts (the RM3100)
that column is the SPI command byte with the read bit already set, and
using it as an I²C sub-address addresses a register that does not exist.

The helpers are @code{static inline}, so each driver still issues its
own single @code{ioctl()} per transfer --- which is exactly what the
mock bus (@code{test/bus_mock.c}, @code{--wrap=ioctl}) intercepts. Keep
any new I/O on this path; SMBus calls, @code{I2C_SLAVE}, or
@code{read()}/@code{write()} would bypass the mock.

@node Writing an IMU driver imu_ops_t
@section Writing an IMU driver (imu_ops_t)
@anchor{#writing-an-imu-driver-imu_ops_t}

@menu
* Static driver state::
* probebus → 0 or -1::
* resetbus → 0 or -1::
* initbus cfg → 0 or -1::
* readbus buf max *n_out → 0 1 or -1::
* Hardware timestamps has_hw_timestamp::
@end menu

@node Static driver state
@subsection Static driver state
@anchor{#static-driver-state}
Because the daemon runs exactly one IMU at a time, each driver stores
its runtime state in a file-scoped static struct:

@verbatim
static struct {
    float    accel_scale;       /* LSB → m/s² */
    float    gyro_scale;        /* LSB → rad/s */
    uint32_t seq;               /* monotonic sample counter */
    uint32_t ticks_per_sample;  /* chip timer ticks between samples (0 if none) */
} s;
@end verbatim

@code{seq} is a monotonic counter incremented for every sample produced.
It must @strong{never reset} while the daemon runs --- the fusion thread
uses gaps in @code{seq} to detect dropped samples.

@node probebus → 0 or -1
@subsection probe(bus) → 0 or -1
@anchor{#probebus--0-or--1}
Read the WHO_AM_I (or equivalent) register and verify it against the
datasheet value. Log a clear error with the received and expected values
on mismatch.

@verbatim
static int myimu_probe(const imud_bus_t *bus)
{
    uint8_t who;
    if (bus_reg_read(bus, REG_WHO_AM_I, &who) < 0) {
        LOG_E("myimu: WHO_AM_I read failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        return -1;
    }
    if (who != WHO_AM_I_EXPECTED) {
        LOG_E("myimu: WHO_AM_I = 0x%02X, expected 0x%02X\n",
              who, WHO_AM_I_EXPECTED);
        return -1;
    }
    return 0;
}
@end verbatim

Use the @code{LOG_E} / @code{LOG_W} / @code{LOG_I} macros from
@code{include/log.h} for all driver output, not bare @code{fprintf} ---
they respect the operator's @code{logging.level}.

@node resetbus → 0 or -1
@subsection reset(bus) → 0 or -1
@anchor{#resetbus--0-or--1}
Trigger a software reset and wait for the bit to self-clear. Always add
the chip's specified power-on time after the reset bit clears ---
skipping this causes init failures on slower hardware.

Not every part has a reset bit. The RM3100 has none at all, so its
@code{reset()} restores the power-on register values by hand; if yours
is like that, say so in the function's comment rather than leaving the
absence to look like an oversight.

@verbatim
static int myimu_reset(const imud_bus_t *bus)
{
    if (bus_reg_write(bus, REG_CTRL, 0x01) < 0) return -1;   /* SW_RESET */
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        usleep(1000);
        uint8_t val;
        if (bus_reg_read(bus, REG_CTRL, &val) < 0) return -1;
        if (!(val & 0x01)) goto done;
    }
    LOG_W("myimu: SW_RESET did not clear after 50 ms\n");
    return -1;
done:
    usleep(20000);   /* chip startup time from datasheet */
    return 0;
}
@end verbatim

@node initbus cfg → 0 or -1
@subsection init(bus, cfg) → 0 or -1
@anchor{#initbus-cfg--0-or--1}
Configure ODR, full-scale range, FIFO mode (if applicable), and
interrupt routing. Save the resulting sensitivity values to the static
@code{s} struct. @code{cfg->odr_hz} is already resolved: the daemon asks
your driver what rate it will really program (see
@ref{The actual_odr_hz hook,,actual_odr_hz} below) and passes that value
back here, so your own rounding is a no-op on it. Round the same way
@code{actual_odr_hz} reports --- by default, up to the next supported
rate.

@verbatim
static int myimu_init(const imud_bus_t *bus, const imu_cfg_t *cfg)
{
    float accel_scale, gyro_scale;
    uint8_t odr  = odr_encode(cfg->odr_hz);
    uint8_t xlfs = xl_fs_encode(cfg->accel_g,  &accel_scale);
    uint8_t gyfs = gy_fs_encode(cfg->gyro_dps, &gyro_scale);

    if (bus_reg_write(bus, REG_ACCEL_CFG, (odr << 4) | xlfs) < 0) return -1;
    if (bus_reg_write(bus, REG_GYRO_CFG,  (odr << 4) | gyfs) < 0) return -1;
    /* ... FIFO, interrupt config ... */

    s.accel_scale = accel_scale;
    s.gyro_scale  = gyro_scale;
    s.seq         = 0;
    return 0;
}
@end verbatim

@node readbus buf max *n_out → 0 1 or -1
@subsection read(bus, buf, max, *n_out) → 0, 1, or -1
@anchor{#readbus-buf-max-n_out--0-1-or--1}
The hot path --- called at the configured ODR. Fill @code{buf[]} with up
to @code{max} calibrated @code{imu_sample_t} samples, set @code{*n_out}
to the number produced, and return:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.09 0.91 
@headitem 
Return
 @tab Meaning
@item 
@code{0}
 @tab Success
@item 
@code{1}
 @tab Success, but a @strong{FIFO overflow} was detected (data gap)
@item 
@code{-1}
 @tab I²C error
@end multitable

@strong{For FIFO-based chips} (recommended --- reduces I²C traffic):

@enumerate 
@item
Read FIFO status to get word count and overflow flag.
@item
Burst-read all pending words.
@item
Parse and scale each word into @code{imu_sample_t}.
@item
Assign @code{seq} and @code{chip_ts}.
@end enumerate

@strong{For non-FIFO chips} (DRDY polling):

@enumerate 
@item
Check DRDY; return @code{*n_out = 0} if not ready.
@item
Burst-read the output registers.
@item
Produce exactly one sample per call.
@end enumerate

Each @code{imu_sample_t} must have:

@verbatim
buf[i].accel[3]  /* m/s², calibrated */
buf[i].gyro[3]   /* rad/s, calibrated (bias NOT subtracted — MEKF does that) */
buf[i].temp_c    /* die temperature, °C */
buf[i].seq       /* s.seq++ — monotonic, never resets */
buf[i].chip_ts   /* see Hardware timestamps below */
@end verbatim

@node Hardware timestamps has_hw_timestamp
@subsection Hardware timestamps (has_hw_timestamp)
@anchor{#hardware-timestamps-has_hw_timestamp}
Set @code{has_hw_timestamp = true} only if your chip has an internal
sample timer that increments at a @strong{fixed rate independent of the
I²C clock}.

@code{ts_tick_ns} is the datasheet's nominal period, and it is fine for
it to be only nominal: the daemon measures the counter's real period
against the host's @code{CLOCK_MONOTONIC} across consecutive 60 s
anchors and uses the measurement in place of your constant (see
@code{ts_anchor_t} in @code{include/imu_math.h}). That is not a
refinement --- a measured ISM330DHCX ran 4.08% fast, so trusting the
constant put sample timestamps seconds out and scaled every integrated
rotation by the same 4%. What the driver must get right is the
@emph{tick}, not its exact duration: the counter has to advance
monotonically at a rate that does not depend on bus traffic or ODR. Get
the period roughly right (within 10%, or the measurement is rejected as
implausible) and the daemon handles the rest.

If your part can say what @emph{its own} timer period is, implement the
optional @code{ts_tick_ns_actual} hook and hand it back. The daemon
calls it once after @code{init()}, with the bus open, and uses the
answer everywhere it would have used your constant. Return 0 --- on a
failed read, or when there is nothing to ask --- and the declared value
stands. This does not replace the runtime measurement above; it fixes
the @emph{first minute}, before two anchors exist. On a part several
percent off nominal that window is not cosmetic: the extrapolated sample
time drifts far enough from the host clock that the sample-latency
histogram stops recording. The ST 6-axis parts carry the answer in
@code{INTERNAL_FREQ_FINE} (0x63), and @code{src/drivers/st_freq_fine.h}
does the arithmetic for all of them.

The ISM330DHCX has a 32-bit counter at 40000 ticks/s (25 µs/tick). If
your chip has an equivalent:

@enumerate 
@item
Read the counter once after draining the FIFO.
@item
Back-calculate per-sample timestamps by stepping back by
@code{ticks_per_sample = clock_rate / odr_hz} per sample:
@end enumerate

@verbatim
uint32_t burst_ts = read_chip_counter();
for (int i = 0; i < produced; i++) {
    uint32_t age = (uint32_t)(produced - 1 - i) * s.ticks_per_sample;
    buf[i].chip_ts = burst_ts - age;   /* 32-bit wrapping arithmetic is correct */
}
@end verbatim

If your chip has @strong{no hardware timer}, set
@code{has_hw_timestamp = false} and leave @code{chip_ts = 0} in every
sample. The anchor mechanism in @code{imu.c} handles this by updating
the wall-clock anchor on every burst.

@node Writing a magnetometer driver mag_ops_t
@section Writing a magnetometer driver (mag_ops_t)
@anchor{#writing-a-magnetometer-driver-mag_ops_t}

@menu
* probe reset init::
* readbus *out → 0 1 or -1::
* Check whether your part's status bit survives the interrupt::
* set_reset optional::
* degauss optional diagnostic::
* has_interrupt::
@end menu

@node probe reset init
@subsection probe, reset, init
@anchor{#probe-reset-init}
Same pattern as the IMU driver. @code{init} configures the ODR and
enables continuous measurement. If the chip has an interrupt pin, enable
it during @code{init} so the mag reader thread can wake on a GPIO edge
rather than polling.

@node readbus *out → 0 1 or -1
@subsection read(bus, *out) → 0, 1, or -1
@anchor{#readbus-out--0-1-or--1}
@multitable @columnfractions 0.09 0.91 
@headitem 
Return
 @tab Meaning
@item 
@code{0}
 @tab Sample written to @code{*out}, @code{out->valid = true}
@item 
@code{1}
 @tab Measurement not complete yet (DRDY not asserted)
@item 
@code{-1}
 @tab I²C error
@end multitable

@node Check whether your part's status bit survives the interrupt
@subsection Check whether your part's status bit survives the interrupt
@anchor{#check-whether-your-parts-status-bit-survives-the-interrupt}
Gating @code{read()} on a "data ready" status bit is the obvious
implementation and it is right for most parts. On some it is not, and
the failure is quiet: the driver works perfectly when polled and
delivers a fraction of the sample rate when the daemon waits on the
interrupt --- which is the only mode the daemon uses.

It goes wrong when @strong{DRDY is a latched interrupt whose acknowledge
write also clears the status bit}. Acknowledging is what re-arms the
edge, so it cannot be skipped; and once it has also taken the status bit
away, a reader that blocks on the edge --- and therefore stops touching
the bus --- may never see the bit come back. Every wake is then spent on
a read that reports "no data", and since the interrupt is latched there
is no second edge to recover with: the reader waits out its timeout for
every single sample.

Ask the datasheet two questions before you gate:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.15 0.67 0.18 
@headitem 
Part
 @tab How DRDY clears
 @tab Safe to gate?
@item 
LIS3MDL
 @tab @code{ZYXDA} is a plain status flag; there is no acknowledge write
 @tab yes
@item 
LIS2MDL
 @tab the DRDY pin @emph{is} the @code{Zyxda} bit, driven straight out
 @tab yes
@item 
RM3100
 @tab the pin "is set LOW when the Measurement Result registers are
read"
 @tab yes
@item 
@strong{MMC5983MA}
 @tab @strong{write 1 to @code{Meas_M_Done} --- which is also the gate
bit}
 @tab @strong{no}
@end multitable

Only the MMC5983MA among the supported parts answers badly, and it was
measured doing so: 35 Hz delivered from a part converting at 105.5 Hz.

When a part is like this, set @code{int_driven} in @code{mag_cfg_t}. The
daemon sets it whenever it has an interrupt line for the mag, and
@code{read()} should then trust the edge instead of the status bit,
still perform the acknowledge write, and reject a sample whose output
registers have not changed --- that last part is what keeps a broken
interrupt line from feeding duplicates to the filter. Polling callers
(@code{imud-cal}, @code{imud-imutest}) leave it false and keep the gate,
which is correct, because a poller has no edge to trust.

@code{imud-imutest}'s @code{mag.drdy.rate} check exists to catch this:
it measures the mag over its interrupt line and compares against the
polled @code{mag.rate}. A driver that gates when it should not shows up
as a large gap between the two.

Always set @code{out->wall_ns} from @code{CLOCK_REALTIME} at read time:

@verbatim
struct timespec ts;
clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);
out->wall_ns = (uint64_t)ts.tv_sec * 1000000000ULL + (uint64_t)ts.tv_nsec;
out->valid   = true;
@end verbatim

Set @code{out->valid = false} and return 0 (not -1) only when the
hardware signals a measurement overflow or other non-fatal data-quality
issue.

@node set_reset optional
@subsection set_reset (optional)
@anchor{#set_reset-optional}
Some magnetometers have a SET/RESET degaussing coil that restores the
AMR sensing elements after exposure to a strong field. If your chip has
one, implement @code{set_reset} and set @code{has_set_reset = true}; the
function issues the pulse and sleeps for the settling time. Set
@code{set_reset = NULL} and @code{has_set_reset = false} if there is no
coil (e.g. AK09916).

Write the whole control register, not just the pulse bit. On parts where
the pulse bits share a register with persistent mode bits --- the
MMC5983MA's CTRL0 carries @code{INT_en} beside @code{Set} and
@code{Reset} --- writing the pulse alone clears the mode bits
@code{init()} set, and the symptom is silent: the interrupt line simply
stops and the reader falls back to polling.

@node degauss optional diagnostic
@subsection degauss (optional, diagnostic)
@anchor{#degauss-optional-diagnostic}
If the coil can be driven both ways, also implement
@code{degauss(bus, MAG_DEGAUSS_SET | MAG_DEGAUSS_RESET)} --- the same
pulse, with the direction chosen by the caller. Leave it @code{NULL}
otherwise; nothing requires it, and @code{set_reset} stays the
production path either way.

It exists because SET and RESET magnetise the film in opposite
directions, so the field term of a reading flips sign between them while
the bridge's own offset does not:

@verbatim
vS = +S*B + offset      (after SET)
vR = -S*B + offset      (after RESET)
  field  = (vS - vR) / 2
  offset = (vS + vR) / 2
@end verbatim

@code{imud-imutest --passive} uses that to separate a genuine external
field from a bridge offset, which is otherwise indistinguishable without
a second transport or a known reference field. Implement it in terms of
one shared helper with @code{set_reset}, so the two cannot drift apart,
and leave the part in the SET state when done.

@node has_interrupt
@subsection has_interrupt
@anchor{#has_interrupt}
Set @code{has_interrupt = true} if the chip asserts an external
interrupt pin on measurement complete --- the mag reader thread requests
a GPIO edge on @code{[mag] int_gpio} and calls @code{read()} on each
rising edge. Set @code{false} if there is no interrupt pin (e.g. AK09916
in bypass mode); the reader falls back to a rate-sized poll --- one
sample period plus @code{int_grace}, so it tracks the configured ODR
rather than a fixed interval.

@node The supported_* tables
@section The supported_* tables
@anchor{#the-supported_-tables}
Zero-terminated ascending integer arrays telling the daemon which rates
your chip supports. @code{imu.c} resolves the operator's requested rate
against these before it reaches your @code{init()} --- see the next
section.

@verbatim
const imu_ops_t myimu_ops = {
    .name               = "myimu",
    /* ... function pointers ... */
    .has_fifo           = true,
    .has_hw_timestamp   = false,
    .supported_odr_hz   = { 12, 26, 52, 104, 208, 416, 833, 0 },
    .supported_accel_g  = { 2, 4, 8, 16, 0 },
    .supported_gyro_dps = { 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 0 },
};
@end verbatim

The last element must be @code{0} (sentinel); keep the list ascending.

@node The actual_odr_hz hook
@section The actual_odr_hz hook
@anchor{#the-actual_odr_hz-hook}

@verbatim
int (*actual_odr_hz)(int requested);
@end verbatim

The rate your driver will really program for @code{requested}, in Hz.
The daemon calls this @strong{before} @code{init()} and passes the
answer back as @code{cfg->odr_hz}, so one configured rate means one rate
everywhere: the chip samples at it, the MEKF's noise variances are sized
for it, and @code{imud-imutest} measures against it. Getting this wrong
tunes the filter for a rate the hardware is not running at.

@strong{Leave it @code{NULL} if your chip has a fixed rate table} ---
which is the usual case. The default rule is then the lowest entry in
@code{supported_odr_hz} at or above @code{requested}, clamped to the
highest, and your @code{odr_encode()} chain must round the same way (a
@code{hz <= X} ladder over the same values does exactly this).

@strong{Implement it if your chip is divider-based}, where the reachable
rates are not the table:

@verbatim
/* ODR = 1000 / (1 + SMPLRT_DIV): the DIVIDER is what rounds, so this part
 * reaches rates that are in no table — 137 Hz becomes 1000/7 = 142. */
static int myimu_actual_odr_hz(int requested)
{
    return odr_actual(smplrt_div_encode(requested));
}
@end verbatim

Also implement it --- as the identity --- if your driver honours
whatever rate it is handed rather than snapping to a grid, as the
@code{sim} driver does.

Resolve through @code{odr_actual_imu()} / @code{odr_actual_mag()} in
@code{imu_math.h} rather than calling the hook directly; that is where
the @code{NULL} default lives. @code{nearest_odr()} still exists but
answers a different question --- "which advertised rate did the operator
probably mean" --- and must not be used to decide tuning.

@node Registering the driver
@section Registering the driver
@anchor{#registering-the-driver}
Edit @code{src/drivers.c} --- two additions per driver:

@verbatim
/* 1. Forward declaration */
extern const imu_ops_t myimu_ops;

/* 2. Pointer in the registry array */
static const imu_ops_t *imu_registry[] = {
    &ism330dhcx_ops,
    &icm20948_ops,
    &myimu_ops,       /* ← add here */
    &sim_imu_ops,
    NULL,
};
@end verbatim

Add the source file to the @code{DRIVER_SRCS} list in the
@code{Makefile}, then enable your chip in the config:

@verbatim
[imu]
driver   = "myimu"
i2c_addr = 0x68
int_gpio = 17
odr_hz   = 500
@end verbatim

If the chip is untested on real hardware, set @code{experimental = true}
in its ops struct so the daemon warns at startup.

@node Testing without hardware --- the sim driver
@section Testing without hardware --- the sim driver
@anchor{#testing-without-hardware--the-sim-driver}
@code{src/drivers/sim.c} implements both @code{sim_imu_ops} and
@code{sim_mag_ops}. It simulates a small boat under way, yawing at 6°/s
from a 60° start heading with wave-induced roll and pitch. To exercise
the full pipeline before hardware arrives:

@verbatim
[imu]
driver   = "sim"
i2c_addr = 0x00
int_gpio = 0          # 0 disables GPIO — reader polls at the batch period

[mag]
driver   = "sim"
i2c_addr = 0x00
int_gpio = 0
@end verbatim

Expected steady-state output:

@verbatim
pitch        ≈ ±2°  (8 s period)
roll         ≈ ±4°  (6 s period)
rate_of_turn ≈ 360 deg/min (with wave-induced oscillation)
heading      increases ~6°/s from a 60° start
@end verbatim

@node Pre-submission checklist
@section Pre-submission checklist
@anchor{#pre-submission-checklist}
@itemize
@item
☐ @code{probe()} reads and validates the chip identity register.
@item
☐ @code{reset()} waits for the reset bit to self-clear @strong{and} adds
the datasheet startup time afterward --- or, on a part with no reset
bit, restores the power-on register values and says so in a comment.
@item
☐ @code{init()} stores sensitivity values to the static struct before
returning.
@item
☐ @code{read()} returns @code{-1} only on I²C errors, never on "no data
yet".
@item
☐ Accelerometer output is m/s² in the NED-compatible board frame (flat
component-up reads ≈ −9.81 on Z).
@item
☐ Gyroscope output is rad/s; magnetometer output is µT.
@item
☐ Z-axis sign flip applied if chip Z is opposite the board IMU Z.
@item
☐ @code{seq} is incremented for every sample, never reset.
@item
☐ @code{chip_ts = 0} and @code{has_hw_timestamp = false} if there is no
hardware timer.
@item
☐ @code{supported_odr_hz[]} is zero-terminated and ascending.
@item
☐ @code{odr_encode()} rounds @strong{up} over exactly
@code{supported_odr_hz[]} --- or @code{actual_odr_hz} is implemented and
reports what the chip really programs.
@item
☐ Driver added to the @code{src/drivers.c} registry and the
@code{Makefile}.
@item
☐ Logs use @code{LOG_*}, not bare @code{fprintf}.
@item
☐ Tested with @code{driver = "sim"} and @code{make test} passes.
@item
☐ Tested on real hardware with @code{imud-status} confirming sensor
activity.
@item
☐ @code{imud-imutest --all} run against the part, with the report
attached to the pull request or issue. This is what clears
@code{experimental = true}.
@end itemize

@bye
